"1. Commun Med (Lond). 2025 Nov 18;6(1):10. doi: 10.1038/s43856-025-01243-5.\n\nThird-generation Cephalosporin resistance in Sub-Saharan Africa: a systematic \nreview and meta-analysis.\n\nGuido G(1), Frallonardo L(2), Asaduzzaman M(3)(4), Farkas FB(5)(6), De Vita \nE(2), Seni A(7), Cotugno S(2), Papagni R(2), Gomonda E(7), Segala FV(2), \nNicastri E(8), Gobbi F(9)(10), Nellums LB(11), Iatta R(12), Chhaganlal K(7), \nVeronese N(13), Putoto G(14), Torti C(15), Saracino A(2), Di Gennaro F(2).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Precision and Regenerative Medicine and Ionian Area \n(DiMePRe-J), Clinic of Infectious Diseases, University of Bari \"Aldo Moro\", \nBari, Italy. giacguido@gmail.com.\n(2)Department of Precision and Regenerative Medicine and Ionian Area \n(DiMePRe-J), Clinic of Infectious Diseases, University of Bari \"Aldo Moro\", \nBari, Italy.\n(3)Department of Community Medicine and Global Health, Institute of Health and \nSociety, Faculty of Medicine, University of Oslo, Oslo, Norway.\n(4)Regional representative of ACGH in Europe, American Society of Tropical \nMedicine and Hygiene (ASTMH), Arlington, VA, USA.\n(5)Department of Medical Microbiology, Faculty of Medicine, Semmelweis \nUniversity, Budapest, Hungary.\n(6)Pediatric Center, Semmelweis University, Budapest, Hungary.\n(7)Department of Research, Faculty of Health Sciences, Universidade Catolica de \nMocambique, Beira, Mozambique.\n(8)National Institute for Infectious Diseases 'Lazzaro Spallanzani' IRCCS, Rome, \nItaly.\n(9)Department of Infectious-Tropical Diseases and Microbiology, IRCCS Sacro \nCuore Don Calabria Hospital, Verona, Italy.\n(10)Department of Clinical and Experimental Sciences, University of Brescia, \nBrescia, Italy.\n(11)College of Population Health, The University of New Mexico, Albuquerque, NM, \nUSA.\n(12)Interdisciplinary Department of Medicine, University of Bari, Bari, Italy.\n(13)Saint Camillus International University of Health Sciences, Rome, Italy.\n(14)Operational Research Unit Doctors with Africa CUAMM, Padova, Italy.\n(15)Dipartimento di Sicurezza e Bioetica - Sezione di Malattie Infettive, \nUniversit\u00e0 Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Rome, Italy.\n\nBACKGROUND: Antimicrobial resistance is a critical global health threat, with \nSub-Saharan Africa disproportionately affected. Third-generation cephalosporins \nare widely used for severe infections due to their safety, broad spectrum, and \neffectiveness. However, resistance to third-generation cephalosporins undermines \ntreatment and exacerbates poor outcomes in regions with limited alternative \ntherapies. This systematic review and meta-analysis evaluated the prevalence, \ntemporal trends, and geographical variations of resistant pathogens in \nSub-Saharan Africa.\nMETHODS: We searched three databases from the inception until 16th August 2024 \nfor observational studies reporting third-generation cephalosporin resistant \ninfections in Sub-Saharan Africa. Eligible studies reported prevalence data for \nconfirmed infections in SSA. Pooled prevalence estimates with 95% confidence \nintervals (CIs) were calculated using random-effects meta-analysis, with \nsubgroup analyses by pathogen, region, clinical setting, and publication year.\nRESULTS: Here we show a total of 200 studies involving 355,136 patients. The \npooled prevalence of third-generation cephalosporin resistant pathogens is 45.3% \n(95% CI: 36.7-54.4%). Resistance rates are highest for Klebsiella pneumoniae \n(57.7%) and Enterobacter spp. (54.2%). Temporal trends show an increase in \nresistance, with prevalence rising from 22.8% before 2009 to 42.0% in 2020-2024. \nGeographical disparities are evident, with higher prevalence in East (59.7%) and \nWest Africa (49.8%) compared to Central Africa (27.0%). High-risk settings \ninclude neonatal intensive care units, surgical wards, and rural or mixed-care \nfacilities.\nCONCLUSIONS: Third-generation cephalosporin resistant pathogens represent an \nescalating global health crisis in SSA, exacerbated by regional disparities and \nrising resistance rates over time. Immediate interventions are needed, including \nenhanced surveillance and antimicrobial stewardship programs.\n\nPlain Language Summary: Antimicrobial resistance occurs when bacteria develop \nthe ability to withstand the effects of antibiotics, making common infections \nincreasingly difficult to treat. This is a serious problem worldwide and \nespecially in sub-Saharan Africa, where population often have limited access to \neffective medicines. We reviewed results from 200 studies including more than \n350,000 patients to see how common this problem is. We found that almost half of \nserious infections caused by Gram-negative bacteria no longer respond to \nthird-generation cephalosporins, which are among the most important antibiotics \nused for severe illness. Resistance is highest in East and West Africa and has \nincreased over the last 20 years, particularly in hospitals and neonatal units. \nThese results show the urgent need for better infection control, careful use of \nantibiotics, and new treatment options.\n\n\u00a9 2025. The Author(s).\n\nDOI: 10.1038/s43856-025-01243-5\nPMCID: PMC12775075\nPMID: 41258441\n\nConflict of interest statement: Competing interests: The authors declare no \ncompeting interests."